  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low pressure continues to depart through this \nafternoon resulting in at least partial clearing. A cold front moves \nthrough tonight and could bring a few showers as this occurs. \n\nLow pressure was centered near Long Island as of 2 PM this \nafternoon. The system will continue to move away with skies becoming \npartly to mostly sunny for the remainder of the afternoon. \nTemperatures are seasonable but dew points are elevated making it \nfeel muggy out. \n\nAs we head into tonight, the low continues to pull away as it moves \nfrom New England into the Canadian Maritimes. This will help pull a \nsecondary front through the area from north to south overnight. As \nthis occurs there could be a few showers and perhaps even a \nthunderstorm but the overall trend should be for showers/storms with \nthe front to be diminishing in coverage as the front pushes south \ninto our area. Any associated severe weather should also remain to \nour north. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2....High pressure builds in to start the week and will \ndominate through at least Wednesday bringing fair and dry weather. \n\nHigh pressure builds in from the west through Monday and Monday \nnight bringing mainly clear skies and temperatures a bit below \naverage for this time of year. Generally expect highs in the 70s \nwith a few places across south Jersey into Delmarva potentially \ngetting to around 80. Northerly winds will also advect in much lower \ndew points making it feel quite comfortable. \n\nHigh pressure will remain centered near the area through Tuesday \nbefore starting to slowly move off the coast heading into Wednesday. \nBoth days look to feature continuing partly to mostly sunny skies \nwith a bit of a warming trend by Wednesday along with some increase \nin humidity as the winds turn more southerly. Expect highs by \nWednesday generally in the upper 70s to low 80s except a bit cooler \nright near the coast and over the Pocono Plateau. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A cold front Thursday into Friday may bring some \nshowers and thunderstorms.\n\nAn upper-level trough across central into eastern Canada is forecast \nto push a cold front into our area at the end of next week. While \nthe guidance, including ensembles, are in decent agreement showing \nthis trough, there are model differences with the amplification and \ntherefore speed of the trough. This will have impacts to the cold \nfront itself. If the main energy slides far enough north of our \narea, the front may tend to be slower moving. This scenario would \nopen the door for a greater chance for a round of convection. A \nfaster movement to the front would lessen the length of convection \nover our area. The front may then linger in the general vicinity of \nthe area next weekend which could keep conditions unsettled at \ntimes. Depending on how much moisture gets drawn northward along the \nfront will determine the heavy rain / flood threat for late week \ninto next weekend. At this point there appears to be at least some \nthreat but the details should come into better focus over the next \nfew days.",
